WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.382
The project asks students to build a small Spark SQL pipeline.

00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:07.512
First, inspect the source schema and grain.

00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:10.657
Second, clean and type the needed columns.

00:00:10.657 --> 00:00:15.322
Third, summarize monthly product revenue or readmission intervals.

00:00:15.322 --> 00:00:18.917
Finally, validate output grain and totals.

00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:25.082
In Spark, validation is still essential because distributed execution can make mistakes expensive.

00:00:25.082 --> 00:00:31.017
Connect this idea to a retail pipeline aggregating high-volume sales data across partitions.

00:00:31.017 --> 00:00:36.232
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.232 --> 00:00:43.467
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:48.692
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:48.692 --> 00:00:53.840
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
